Venetium 0043 - ARM32 renderer lockdown token USER_LIMITED (fixes the sandboxed-renderer DLL-load death on Windows 10 ARM32 /
Tegra, build 15035). PROVEN cause (his device, 2026-10-07): Windows 10's parallel module loader maps a process's DLLs on
LoaderWorker threads that run under the process PRIMARY token. Chromium creates the renderer with the USER_LOCKDOWN primary token
(its only restricting SID is the NULL SID) and impersonates the permissive initial token on the MAIN thread only (broker_services.cc),
which the loader worker threads do not share -- so every DLL file open on a worker thread fails and the renderer dies before it can
log. Setting IFEO MaxLoaderThreads=1 for venetium.exe (serial loading) makes pages load, confirming the cause; but serial loading
slows every process startup noticeably on Tegra, so this patch keeps the parallel loader fully enabled and instead lowers ONLY the
ARM32 renderer's lockdown token to USER_LIMITED -- the exact token the GPU process already runs with successfully on this hardware
(content/browser/gpu/gpu_process_host.cc: "we gotten as far as USER_LIMITED; USER_RESTRICTED breaks DirectX+OpenGL"). USER_LIMITED's
restricting SIDs (Users/World/RESTRICTED/logon) let the loader worker threads read the application and system DLLs, while still
enforcing a real restricted-token sandbox (integrity stays LOW -> delayed UNTRUSTED, lockdown job, win32k lockdown, all mitigations).
Weaker than USER_LOCKDOWN, far stronger than --no-sandbox. The override is placed in the renderer delegate's InitializeConfig, which
sandbox_win.cc invokes AFTER AddDefaultConfigForSandboxedProcess() has set USER_LOCKDOWN, so the second SetTokenLevel() cleanly
overrides it (ConfigBase::SetTokenLevel only stores the levels and checks initial>=lockdown). Guarded by ARCH_CPU_ARMEL so the x86
build keeps USER_LOCKDOWN. Supersedes 0038 (its S-1-5-12 app-dir grant was built on the wrong premise and is unnecessary under
USER_LIMITED, where Users/World already read the app dir); 0038 is dropped from the series.

--- a/content/browser/renderer_host/renderer_sandboxed_process_launcher_delegate.cc
+++ b/content/browser/renderer_host/renderer_sandboxed_process_launcher_delegate.cc
@@ -108,6 +108,31 @@ bool RendererSandboxedProcessLauncherDelegateWin::InitializeConfig(
     sandbox::TargetConfig* config) {
   DCHECK(!config->IsConfigured());

+#if defined(ARCH_CPU_ARMEL)
+  // Venetium 0043: Windows 10 ARM32 (Tegra, build 15035) maps a process's
+  // DLLs on parallel loader worker threads that run under the process PRIMARY
+  // token. Chromium creates the renderer with the USER_LOCKDOWN primary token
+  // (its only restricting SID is the NULL SID) and impersonates the permissive
+  // initial token on the MAIN thread only -- which the loader worker threads
+  // do not share. Every DLL file open on a worker thread therefore fails and
+  // the renderer dies before it can log. Give the ARM32 renderer the
+  // USER_LIMITED lockdown token instead: it is the exact token the GPU process
+  // already runs with successfully on this hardware (see gpu_process_host.cc),
+  // and its restricting SIDs (Users/World/RESTRICTED/logon) let the loader
+  // worker threads read the application and system DLLs while still enforcing a
+  // real restricted-token sandbox. This overrides the USER_LOCKDOWN set by
+  // AddDefaultConfigForSandboxedProcess(), which runs before this delegate
+  // hook. Preferred over disabling the parallel loader, which serializes
+  // process startup and is noticeably slow on Tegra.
+  {
+    const sandbox::ResultCode result = config->SetTokenLevel(
+        sandbox::USER_RESTRICTED_SAME_ACCESS, sandbox::USER_LIMITED);
+    if (result != sandbox::SBOX_ALL_OK) {
+      return false;
+    }
+  }
+#endif  // defined(ARCH_CPU_ARMEL)
+
   sandbox::policy::SandboxWin::AddBaseHandleClosePolicy(config);

   ContentBrowserClient::AppContainerFlags ac_flags(
